WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.128 --> 00:00:05.755
I think that on campuses, buildings teach.

00:00:06.506 --> 00:00:09.092
I think they teach the students

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:14.806
subliminally about the value of place
 and architecture and craft

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:18.852
and this building
 would be a great representation of that.

00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:22.272
A building like this
 is a sensory building.

00:00:22.272 --> 00:00:26.568
The wood has its own aroma
and color and richness.

00:00:26.568 --> 00:00:28.570
It has light.

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:30.655
It has its own sound. 

00:00:30.655 --> 00:00:34.200
And it already has its own mythology.

00:00:34.617 --> 00:00:37.912
It has many stories to tell. 

00:00:37.912 --> 00:00:40.957
It would be impossible
 to come in this building, spend time here,

00:00:40.957 --> 00:00:43.626
and not remember it. 

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:49.299
This building is a very iconic building

00:00:50.467 --> 00:00:52.302
where it’s situated, close to the edge of campus

00:00:52.302 --> 00:00:55.096
with its tower on the New Haven skyline... 

00:00:55.096 --> 00:00:56.723
It claims itself as a destination. 

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:03.063
The humanities are about research
 in balance with collaboration

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:08.318
and in balance with engagement with
 communities of thinkers and enthusiasts.

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:14.074
The concept here
 was of a unified humanities environment.

00:01:14.699 --> 00:01:18.578
It would have been very difficult
 to casually bump

00:01:18.578 --> 00:01:21.915
into somebody from another department
 in the building the way it was.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.711
It wasn't a building that spoke
 to collaboration and commonality.

00:01:26.711 --> 00:01:27.796
These departments
were scattered. 

00:01:27.796 --> 00:01:28.797
Some of them were here 

00:01:28.797 --> 00:01:32.842
but French and Italian and Spanish was
 over on Wall Street above the pizza place.

00:01:32.842 --> 00:01:36.096
Then if you wanted to go to comp lit,
 you had to go to 451 College.

00:01:36.096 --> 00:01:37.597
German was across the street. 

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.517
You can't build something whole
 when everything is fragmented.

00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.977
You have to be able
 to bring the fragments together.

00:01:42.977 --> 00:01:46.815
We're very well known
 for being a place of the humanities,

00:01:46.815 --> 00:01:49.692
a place that has a singular leadership
 role in the humanities,

00:01:49.692 --> 00:01:52.946
not only in the United States,
 but also around the world.

00:01:52.946 --> 00:01:56.199
Bringing them together into a single location

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:57.909
is really an opportunity

00:01:57.909 --> 00:02:02.038
to draw together
 in a physical way, the commitment

00:02:02.038 --> 00:02:05.458
we have always had in a spiritual way.

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:09.254
The idea of creating common
 ground in a building

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:13.007
that had been rather cellular
 so that there was a flow in the building

00:02:13.007 --> 00:02:14.342
was one of the challenges. 

00:02:14.342 --> 00:02:17.011
This was an incredibly complex process.

00:02:17.011 --> 00:02:21.015
It's a lot of choreography between
 understanding the end users' desires,

00:02:21.015 --> 00:02:23.351
working with the architect
 with design aspirations,

00:02:23.351 --> 00:02:25.061
working with the construction manager 

00:02:25.061 --> 00:02:27.981
for constructability,
 and putting that all together.

00:02:28.606 --> 00:02:33.027
Renovating a building is a lot more
 challenging than building a new building.

00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:37.657
For me, one of the most exciting things
 was thinking how we could make it better.

00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:52.172
There was always a desire to preserve
 the architecture of this building.

00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:53.673
It's such an iconic building. 

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:56.885
James Gamble Rogers
 did many of the buildings on campus

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:59.596
and there's an expectation
 of ornamentation and detail

00:02:59.596 --> 00:03:01.723
that people expect
 when they come into Yale buildings.

00:03:03.516 --> 00:03:07.770
The idea of a larger footprint became
 very appealing because it could offer us

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:13.193
the structural spans to support the big
 collaborative and instructional spaces

00:03:13.193 --> 00:03:17.155
without having to tear apart
 a valued historic building.

00:03:17.155 --> 00:03:21.367
We put in 12,000
 square feet of underground space

00:03:21.367 --> 00:03:22.911
that never existed before. 

00:03:22.911 --> 00:03:26.998
And there's a state of the art lecture
 hall and film screening room down there.

00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:30.376
We have a courtyard that's really a roof
 now for another building.

00:03:30.376 --> 00:03:31.711
The fact that we had to construct 

00:03:31.711 --> 00:03:34.714
below the water table
 and underpin the foundations

00:03:34.714 --> 00:03:38.134
for the entire perimeter of the courtyard,
 including a 15 storey tower.

00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:41.846
We had to dig down 30 feet, dug out thirty
 thousand cubic yards of soil.

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:45.767
Those are just mind-boggling exercises
 in and of themselves.

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.773
The entry sequence
 into this building is very grand.

00:03:51.773 --> 00:03:55.693
You have these beautiful brick arches
 that welcome you right away.

00:03:56.277 --> 00:04:00.198
You can go left or right
 and be in a light filled hallway now

00:04:00.198 --> 00:04:03.743
that is adorned with
offices and classroom space.

00:04:04.661 --> 00:04:09.707
The building brings together
 15 different departments and programs

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:14.671
Near Eastern languages and civilizations
 sits on a floor with directed studies.

00:04:14.712 --> 00:04:16.339
Film and media studies 

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:20.551
sits adjacent to our Department
 of Italian language and literature.

00:04:20.677 --> 00:04:24.180
History is now co-located
 with units that study

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:27.767
the literary traditions
 in each of the regions of the world.

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:29.769
I can work with professors
 and hear the lectures

00:04:29.769 --> 00:04:31.938
that they bring in, work
 with grad students.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:36.150
We took many existing corridors
 and we actually widened them.

00:04:37.068 --> 00:04:43.533
We created spaces where people could sit
 down, have a cup of coffee, see some people.

00:04:43.533 --> 00:04:47.996
These are connective spaces and they're
 intended to be used in a very fluid way.

00:04:47.996 --> 00:04:52.208
Now, they have lots of small little rooms
 for conferences that we can immediately just grab

00:04:52.208 --> 00:04:56.379
another professor, move into a room
 and talk, and widen out the conversation.

00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:01.092
You have an access to the tower,
 which in itself has a beautiful lobby.

00:05:01.134 --> 00:05:06.639
Swenson Tower will be home to graduate
 students from across a range of departments.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:12.770
The new stair that connects the entrance
 foyer to the lowest two levels

00:05:12.812 --> 00:05:16.691
required a complete structural
 rethink of that part of the building.

00:05:16.691 --> 00:05:19.360
It's designed to invite people.

00:05:19.736 --> 00:05:23.239
When I walk to the building
 now, it's so easy to imagine how

00:05:23.239 --> 00:05:26.868
a great lecture could be given
 in the lower level lecture hall.

00:05:26.868 --> 00:05:30.204
And you walk out with someone that you're
 sitting next to and have a conversation

00:05:30.204 --> 00:05:33.166
in the beautiful reception space
 right outside that lecture hall.

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:36.461
Maybe you decide there that it'd be great
 to continue that conversation

00:05:36.461 --> 00:05:38.755
in your offices,
 which are only two floors away.

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:43.426
I think we always strive to bring people
 together in ways that they don't expect.

00:05:43.426 --> 00:05:47.263
And this project
 specifically will do that.

00:05:47.263 --> 00:05:49.432
We're going to have a lot more interaction
 among the faculty.

00:05:49.432 --> 00:05:54.604
And hopefully for the students, they will develop
interests they didn't plan to develop along the way.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.447
Now this building is a building
 that speaks to not only

00:06:04.447 --> 00:06:07.450
the need for privacy and individual research,

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:12.914
but also the opportunity for the chance encounter,   the collaboration, and bringing people together.

00:06:14.082 --> 00:06:18.378
Yale would be the first to admit
 that it hadn't seen much updating or improvement

00:06:18.378 --> 00:06:19.879
in many decades.

00:06:20.380 --> 00:06:23.633
If you look at the plan of the building,
 it's a complex plan.

00:06:23.633 --> 00:06:27.678
The whole basement level, which is now the
 concourse, was loaded with steam piping.

00:06:27.678 --> 00:06:30.098
So we had to be very creative
 about how to route everything

00:06:30.098 --> 00:06:31.557
behind the beautiful architecture. 

00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:35.978
We've probably never met a building
 before that had such resistance

00:06:35.978 --> 00:06:39.482
to upgrade in its structural composition.

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:40.942
There was also the challenge 

00:06:40.942 --> 00:06:44.028
of making the building
 fully ADA compliant, fully accessible.

00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:46.447
That is a big deal. 

00:06:46.447 --> 00:06:48.449
And to try to do that in a way
 that's very sensitive to

00:06:48.449 --> 00:06:51.661
the architecture
 is very challenging as well.

00:06:52.203 --> 00:06:56.374
In order to restore
 a lot of the detail in this building,

00:06:56.374 --> 00:06:59.377
we did rely on really good crafts people,
 many of them local.

00:06:59.919 --> 00:07:01.921
We replaced all the windows. 

00:07:01.921 --> 00:07:05.800
We salvaged two hundred and eighty
 four panels of art glass.

00:07:06.092 --> 00:07:08.177
Most of the millwork
 was carefully restored. 

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:12.140
We had a local vendor
 restoring all the ironwork.

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:13.516
All of the historic lighting 

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:17.437
was sent out to be refurbished
 with energy efficient LED.

00:07:17.437 --> 00:07:20.648
And then there's quite a lot of new
 lighting to supplement that.

00:07:23.943 --> 00:07:25.987
This didn't just happen. 

00:07:25.987 --> 00:07:31.075
This was planned, monitored, and executed

00:07:31.075 --> 00:07:33.911
in ways that took time and care

00:07:33.911 --> 00:07:35.329
and work.

00:07:35.663 --> 00:07:40.168
Structurals, mechanicals, civil, lighting
 designers, the landscape architect...

00:07:40.168 --> 00:07:42.003
You know, there was
 a cast of thousands. 

00:07:42.003 --> 00:07:46.757
Hundreds and hundreds of unique
 individuals worked on this particular project.

00:07:47.383 --> 00:07:49.719
A lot of local New Haven residents. 

00:07:49.719 --> 00:07:53.473
To add it up we say it's
 six hundred and thirty thousand hours

00:07:53.473 --> 00:07:57.310
went into the renovation of this building
 by a wide variety of people.

00:07:57.768 --> 00:08:01.439
You pull these things off because you have
 people who just never give up.

00:08:01.439 --> 00:08:05.860
And that tenacity is part
 of the success story of this project.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:10.448
If there is one thing I'm most proud of,
 it's how well we worked as a team.

00:08:13.367 --> 00:08:16.204
I hope that this
 building will say something about

00:08:16.204 --> 00:08:20.291
optimizing existing conditions
 in ways that are innovative.

00:08:20.291 --> 00:08:23.294
There's the historic nature
 of the building from ground floor up,

00:08:23.294 --> 00:08:27.340
and then there's a much more modern
 intervention from concourse level down.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:30.885
And I hope it'll say something
 about making a building that's available

00:08:30.885 --> 00:08:34.472
to everybody who uses it
 by the way, that it's organized.

00:08:34.472 --> 00:08:37.099
It's James Gamble Rogers 2.0, if you will.

00:08:37.099 --> 00:08:38.851
It's a 21st century building now. 

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:40.478
I hope the students really understand 

00:08:40.478 --> 00:08:42.939
the value of the humanities
 through this building.

00:08:43.356 --> 00:08:48.194
When you walk into it, you can't help but
 feel inspired of what could be possible.

00:08:48.945 --> 00:08:55.826
That we as a university decided to take
one of our central iconic campus buildings

00:08:55.826 --> 00:09:00.206
to rebuild the space in ways
that encourage collaboration

00:09:00.206 --> 00:09:01.290
in the humanities

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:07.463
is a signal to the world that we are
committed to this area of excellence.

